+<a id="retrying-failed-test-cases"></a>
+## Retrying failed test cases
+<pre>--retry-failed &lt;retries&gt;</pre>
+
+> Introduced in Catch2 X.Y.Z
+
+`--retry-failed N` allows up to `N` retries **after** the first attempt of
+each failed test case. Thus the maximum number of attempts for one test case
+is `N + 1`; the budget is per test case, not shared across the test run. The
+default is `0`, and omitting the option is equivalent to
+`--retry-failed 0`.
+
+For example,
+
+```console
+./tests --retry-failed 0  # one attempt, no retries
+./tests --retry-failed 1  # at most two attempts
+./tests --retry-failed 3  # at most four attempts
+```
+
+Catch2 stops at the first attempt whose outcome is not failed. The last
+attempt that runs determines the test case's final result and the process
+exit code. A test that fails one or more attempts and then passes is reported
+as a flaky pass. Its superseded failures remain available as diagnostics,
+but only the final attempt contributes to final assertion and test-case
+totals.
+
+An _attempt_ is a complete execution of a logical test case. It includes
+all _partial runs_ needed to traverse the test's sections and generators.
+The retry decision is made only after those partial runs finish; a partial
+run is not a retry and does not consume the retry budget. A retry starts the
+whole test case again rather than resuming at the failed assertion, section,
+or generator value.
+
+Each attempt starts with fresh Catch2-managed state. Section traversal and
+generators restart, persistent fixtures are reconstructed, assertion and
+output-capture state is reset, and Catch2's random-number generator is
+reseeded with the run's seed. Command-line section, generator, and path
+filters still apply to every attempt. State owned by the test is **not**
+rolled back: this includes globals, statics, files, environment variables,
+databases, network services, and threads started by the test.
+
+Catch2 applies its existing outcome rules before deciding to retry. A failed
+`[!mayfail]` or `[!shouldfail]` test is an accepted failure and is not
+retried. An unexpected pass of a `[!shouldfail]` test is still a failure and
+can be retried. A skip is not retried. Crashes, fatal signals, structured
+exceptions, stack overflows, and forced termination cannot be retried.
+
+The `--abort` and `--abortx` options take precedence over retries. If an
+attempt reaches the abort threshold, it is final and no retry starts. Failed
+assertions from an attempt that is superseded do not count towards the abort
+threshold for later tests. In particular, `--abort` normally prevents a
+failed attempt from being retried because its threshold is one.
+
