+<a id="testcaseattempt-events"></a>
+### `testCaseAttempt` events
+
+> Introduced in Catch2 X.Y.Z
+
+```cpp
+void testCaseAttemptStarting( TestCaseAttemptInfo const& attemptInfo );
+void testCaseAttemptEnded( TestCaseAttemptStats const& attemptStats );
+```
+
+When [`--retry-failed N`](command-line.md#retrying-failed-test-cases) is
+greater than zero, these events bookend every attempt, including attempt 1
+and an attempt that passes immediately. They are nested inside the single
+`testCaseStarting`/`testCaseEnded` pair and outside that attempt's partial
+runs:
+
+```text
+testCaseStarting
+  testCaseAttemptStarting(k = 1, M)
+    testCasePartialStarting ... testCasePartialEnded
+  testCaseAttemptEnded(k = 1, M)
+  testCaseAttemptStarting(k = 2, M)       only after a failed attempt
+    ...
+  testCaseAttemptEnded(k = 2, M)
+testCaseEnded
+```
+
+Partial-run `partNumber` values continue increasing across attempts; they do
+not restart at one and are not attempt identifiers. An attempt stopped by an
+abort threshold is ended as the final attempt. Catch2 also sends the matching
+attempt-end event on its existing fatal-error reporting path before the
+test-case and test-run end events.
+
+`attemptNumber` is one-based and `maxAttempts` is `M = N + 1` in both
+structures. `TestCaseAttemptInfo` also contains `testInfo`.
+`TestCaseAttemptStats` contains the attempt's own `totals`, captured
+`stdOut` and `stdErr`, and `aborting`. Its `isFinal` field is false exactly
+when this attempt is superseded and another attempt follows. The attempt-end
+statistics are authoritative for that attempt; totals attached to assertion
+events are only progress information.
+
+The `TestCaseStats` passed to `testCaseEnded` has three retry-related fields:
+
+* `attemptCount` is the number of attempts that ran;
+* `maxAttempts` is `M`;
+* `isFlaky` is true when one or more failed attempts were followed by a
+  passing final attempt.
+
+Its totals describe only the final attempt, while its captured `stdOut` and
+`stdErr` contain output from all attempts in order. When retries are disabled,
+these fields retain their compatibility defaults (`1`, `1`, and `false`),
+and no attempt events are emitted.
+
+The new event functions have default no-op implementations rather than being
+pure virtual. Existing reporters and listeners therefore keep compiling and
+working without overriding them. Similarly, the default implementation of
+`IConfig::retryFailed()` returns zero. Multi-reporters forward attempt events
+to every listener and reporter in the same order as other events.
+
+
